{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Teaching in Tech-Rich Classrooms With TeachGen@i","title":"Adapting Plans for Learner Variability","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting adapting plans for learner variability to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"05","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/02/04"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Adapting Plans for Learner Variability\n\n![Adapting Plans for Learner Variability textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/02-05-adapting-plans-for-learner-variability.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nLearner variability is the ordinary fact that a single class contains students who decode at different rates, hold different prior knowledge, and reach the work through different languages and supports. Adapting a plan for that variability is not the same as making the work easier. The professional distinction, drawn from [Universal Design for Learning](https://udlguidelines.cast.org/) (CAST, 2024), is that you change the route to a learning goal while holding the goal constant for everyone. A weak adaptation lowers the goal for some students (a simpler text, fewer questions) and, in doing so, teaches them something less than their peers learned. A strong adaptation keeps the goal fixed and removes the specific barrier that stood between a student and that goal. This section trains the diagnostic move at the center of that distinction: locating the actual barrier rather than reacting to a label.\n\nThe artifact, a Design Tension Studio map, records the live tension between equal access and equal expectations, and the negotiated adaptation that protects both. A reviewer should see the same goal surviving for every student while only the access path changed.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nThe objective is fixed for the whole class: \"Students analyze a primary-source letter from 1863.\" Two students read well below grade level, and three multilingual learners are new to dense historical English. The fast, tempting fix is to hand those five a shorter, plainer letter. It feels kind and it is wrong: now five students analyze an easier source while their peers analyze the real one, and the gap the teacher meant to close has quietly widened. The barrier in this room is not analysis ability; it is decoding archaic vocabulary and long sentences. An adaptation that keeps the 1863 letter but removes the decoding barrier serves the goal. An adaptation that swaps the letter abandons it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAdapting for variability means holding the goal constant and changing the access path, which requires you to separate three things students often conflate: the goal, the barrier, and the label.\n\n| Element | What it is | The trap |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Goal | The cognitive task everyone must reach (analyze a source) | Lowering it for some students and calling it differentiation |\n| Barrier | The specific obstacle between this student and the goal (decoding archaic text) | Treating the barrier as if it were a deficit in the goal itself |\n| Label | A category attached to a student (ELL, IEP, below grade level) | Designing from the label instead of the task barrier |\n\nThe decisive habit is to build adaptations from the barrier, not the label. \"ELL\" does not tell you what to change; \"new to dense historical English\" tells you to supply a context preview and a glossary. The same student labeled ELL needs nothing extra for a hands-on lab. Three access levers usually suffice once the barrier is named: change the input (audio, a sentence-by-sentence version, a glossary), change the support (a model, a preview, a partner), or change the output mode (speak instead of write) while holding the cognitive demand identical. [TPACK](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x) (Mishra & Koehler, 2006) and critical edtech studies (Selwyn) add a caution: an AI adaptation tool will often default to lowering the goal because that is the easiest text transformation, so you must check that what it changed was the access, not the analysis; human reading-comprehension evaluations confirm that text-simplification systems do not reliably preserve meaning (Agrawal & Carpuat, 2024).\n\n<inline-check question=\"The goal is to analyze an 1863 letter. Two students read well below grade level. A teacher hands them a shorter, plainer letter. Why is this not a defensible adaptation?\" explanation=\"The barrier is decoding archaic vocabulary and long sentences, not analysis ability. A simpler letter changes the goal: those students now analyze a different, easier source while peers analyze the real one, so the gap widens. A defensible adaptation keeps the same letter and removes the decoding barrier through audio, a glossary, or a sentence-by-sentence version.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It changes the goal, since those students now analyze an easier source\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is fine, because a simpler text is always kinder to struggling readers\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is too much work; fewer questions would be a better adaptation\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one annotated judgment call on adapting for learner variability. The objective is fixed: \"Students analyze a primary-source letter from 1863.\" The class includes two students reading well below grade level and three multilingual learners new to dense historical English.\n\n**Step 1. Keep the goal, find the barrier.** The objective stays analyze a primary source. The barrier is not analysis ability; it is decoding archaic vocabulary and long sentences in the letter.\n\n**Step 2. Reject the easy but wrong moves.**\n\n> **Tempting move A (rejected):** Give struggling readers a shorter, simpler letter. Annotated: changes the goal, since they now analyze a different, easier source.\n\n> **Tempting move B (rejected):** Give them four questions instead of twelve. Annotated: changes only quantity; the decoding barrier is untouched.\n\n**Step 3. Make the access-preserving decision.** Recorded on the map: \"Keep the same 1863 letter and the same analysis questions for everyone. Provide a glossary of six archaic terms, an audio reading of the letter, and a sentence-by-sentence version for the two below-level readers. Multilingual learners get the glossary plus a first-language preview of the historical context.\"\n\n**Step 4. Tie it to the label trap.** The teacher notes: the adaptation was built from the task barrier (archaic decoding), not from the labels ELL or below grade level. Every student still performs the same analysis, so a mentor can see the goal held while access changed.\n\nMatch each named barrier to the access lever that removes it while holding the cognitive demand identical. Notice that the lever follows the barrier, never the label.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Decoding archaic vocabulary in the 1863 letter\",\"definition\":\"Provide a glossary, an audio reading, and a sentence-by-sentence version (change the input)\"},{\"term\":\"New to dense historical English\",\"definition\":\"Provide a first-language preview of the historical context (change the support)\"},{\"term\":\"Dyscalculia blocking multi-digit arithmetic\",\"definition\":\"Allow a calculator so the modeling cognition is not blocked by computation\"},{\"term\":\"Selectively mute child who must retell a story\",\"definition\":\"Let the child order picture cards to show the sequence (change the output mode)\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nHold the goal constant for every student; change only the access path, and build that path from the named barrier rather than from the student's label. If an adaptation changes what students learn rather than how they reach it, it is a lowered goal in disguise.\n\nTest any adaptation against four questions:\n\n1. **Goal:** Is the cognitive demand identical to what every other student faces?\n2. **Barrier:** Have I named the specific obstacle, separate from the label?\n3. **Lever:** Does my change adjust input, support, or output mode, not the task itself?\n4. **Label trap:** Did I design from the barrier, or did I default to a generic \"easier\" version because of a category?\n\nIf the goal moved, the adaptation has failed even if the student is happier.\n\nWalk the 1863-letter decision and watch each choice either hold the goal or quietly lower it.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Adapting the 1863 letter\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"The goal is fixed: every student analyzes a primary-source letter from 1863. Two students read well below grade level and three multilingual learners are new to dense historical English. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Give the five students a shorter, plainer letter\",\"next\":\"swap\",\"feedback\":\"That swaps the source, so those five analyze an easier text. The goal moved; this is a lowered goal in disguise.\"},{\"label\":\"Name the barrier before choosing any change\",\"next\":\"barrier\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The barrier is decoding archaic vocabulary and long sentences, not analysis ability. Diagnose before adapting.\"},{\"label\":\"Build a generic ELL packet for the multilingual learners\",\"next\":\"label\",\"feedback\":\"Designing from the label ignores which specific demand of the current task is hard. The lever should follow the barrier.\"}]},\"swap\":{\"prompt\":\"In the discussion, the five students cannot engage the analysis their peers are doing of the real letter, because they read a different one. How do you correct course?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Restore the same letter and remove the decoding barrier instead\",\"next\":\"barrier\",\"feedback\":\"Hold the goal constant; change only the access path.\"}]},\"label\":{\"prompt\":\"The packet simplifies the prose so much it strips the complexity the objective asked students to analyze. What went wrong?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"You designed from the label, not the barrier; diagnose the specific obstacle\",\"next\":\"barrier\",\"feedback\":\"Same student, hands-on lab, needs nothing extra. The label does not tell you what to change.\"}]},\"barrier\":{\"prompt\":\"Barrier named: decoding archaic text. Which change keeps the analysis demand identical?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep the same letter and questions; add a glossary, an audio reading, and a sentence-by-sentence version\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"You changed the input while every student still performs the same analysis. Access changed, goal held.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep the letter but give those students four questions instead of twelve\",\"next\":\"label\",\"feedback\":\"That changes only quantity; the decoding barrier is untouched and the demand is reduced.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"Your map shows one goal unchanged across students, the specific barrier behind each adaptation, and the reduction move you rejected with its reason.\",\"feedback\":\"Adaptation is barrier removal, not making the work smaller: hold the goal, change the access, build from the barrier not the label.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe first misreading is to equate adaptation with reduction: fewer questions, simpler text, lighter rubric. Reduction usually leaves the real barrier intact while teaching less. The second is to design from the label, producing a one-size \"ELL packet\" that ignores which specific demand of today's task is hard for which student. The third is to assume an AI rewrite preserved the goal. When a teacher pastes a primary source into a general-purpose assistant such as ChatGPT or Gemini and asks it to \"simplify,\" the tool frequently strips the very complexity the objective asked students to analyze, so the simplified version trains a different, lower skill; controlled human evaluations show simplification systems do not reliably preserve the original's meaning (Agrawal & Carpuat, 2024). Always compare the adapted task's demand to the original's before trusting it.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the goal-barrier-lever method to a math word-problem lesson whose objective is \"Students model a real situation with a linear equation.\" A student with dyscalculia and a student new to English both struggle, but for different barriers. The dyscalculia barrier is holding multi-digit arithmetic in working memory; the lever is allowing a calculator so the modeling cognition is not blocked by computation. The language barrier is parsing the problem's English; the lever is a visual diagram of the situation and key vocabulary pre-taught. Both students still write and justify the same linear equation, so the goal is untouched. The map records two different barriers, two different levers, and one shared goal, which is exactly what distinguishes adaptation from giving some students a smaller math problem.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: in the left column write the goal and the barrier; in the right column write the access lever you chose and confirm the goal did not move. For the 1863 letter, the left column says \"analyze the source; barrier is decoding archaic text\" and the right column says \"audio plus glossary; same letter, same questions.\" A strong reader can audit any adaptation by asking, \"did the cognitive demand survive?\" If a student ended up doing easier thinking, not just easier reading, the adaptation crossed the line. Your map is strong when the goal is visibly identical across students.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong adaptation map states the single goal that every student must reach and shows it unchanged across adaptations. It names the specific barrier for each adaptation, separate from any label, so a reviewer can see you diagnosed rather than categorized. It uses access levers (input, support, output mode) and documents that the cognitive demand stayed constant. And it records at least one reduction move you rejected (the shorter text, the fewer questions) with the reason it would have lowered the goal. Polish is a neat differentiation chart; evidence is the demonstrated constancy of the goal beneath the varied access.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability\", \"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the method to a kindergarten setting where the objective is \"Students retell a story in sequence.\" There are no reading levels yet, but variability is real: one child is selectively mute, one is a wheelchair user, one is still building English. The goal-barrier-lever logic transfers, but the levers change shape: the selectively mute child retells by ordering picture cards (output mode), the wheelchair user needs the sequencing materials at an accessible height (physical access), the English learner gets the key sequence words pre-taught (input support). The goal, retell in sequence, holds for all three. If you can design these without reaching for \"easier story,\" you understand adaptation as barrier removal rather than as making the work smaller.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A teammate runs the 1863 letter through an AI tool that simplifies it, then assigns the simplified version to struggling readers and says the adaptation is done. In two or three sentences, explain the risk in trusting that the AI rewrite preserved the goal, and the one check you would run before using it.\" answer=\"When a tool simplifies a primary source it frequently strips the very complexity the objective asked students to analyze, so the simplified version trains a different, lower skill while looking like an adaptation. Trusting the rewrite can quietly lower the goal for some students. Before using it I would compare the adapted task demand to the original: if the analysis the simplified text supports is easier thinking, not just easier reading, the goal moved and the rewrite fails.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/02-05-adapting-plans-for-learner-variability-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"02.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 02.05. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your map shows one goal unchanged across students, names the specific barrier behind each adaptation, and rejects at least one reduction move that would have lowered the goal. Holding the goal while changing access is what separates adaptation from making the work smaller.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). Is technology good for education? Polity Press. ISBN 9780745696461.\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. Sustainability, 16(3). https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- CAST. (2024). *Universal Design for Learning Guidelines version 3.0*. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- Agrawal, S., & Carpuat, M. (2024). Do text simplification systems preserve meaning? A human evaluation via reading comprehension. *Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics, 12*. https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00653\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability","stem":"To adapt a writing lesson for variability, a teacher gives advanced students twelve sentences to write and struggling students four. Why is this a weak adaptation?","options":["Four sentences is still too many for some students.","It changes only the amount of work; the cognitive demand and the access barriers stay identical for everyone.","Advanced students should get fewer sentences, not more.","Writing is not a good subject for differentiation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Adapting for variability changes the path to the objective, not the page count. Adjusting quantity alone leaves the same students stuck.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_differentiation_is_volume","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability","stem":"The objective is \"analyze a primary source.\" For a student who needs reading support, which adaptation keeps the goal intact?","options":["Replace the analysis task with a fill-in-the-blank vocabulary sheet.","Provide the same source with a glossary and an audio version so the student still performs the analysis.","Have the student summarize a children's book instead.","Skip the primary source and grade on participation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An accommodation changes access, not the target. The student still analyzes a primary source; only the route to the text changes.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_lower_the_goal","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher prompts TeachGen@i: \"Adapt this lesson for my ELL students.\" The AI returns a generic ELL accommodation list. What is the most defensible next move?","options":["Apply the whole list, since it is labeled for ELLs.","Name the specific barrier this task creates (for example, dense idioms in the word problems) and adapt to that barrier, not to the label.","Ignore the list because AI cannot help with language learners.","Give those students a shorter version of the same lesson."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A category label is not a lesson plan. The teacher identifies the task-specific barrier for these students and adapts to it.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_label_drives_plan","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"On a Design Tension Studio map about adapting for variability, which entry best documents a defensible decision?","options":["\"Made it easier for some, harder for others.\"","Tension named (one common task vs. unequal access) paired with the decision to keep one objective but offer the source as text, audio, and annotated excerpt, and the reason a quantity-only AI suggestion was rejected for not changing access.","\"Differentiated the lesson.\"","\"Gave the IEP students extra time.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible map names the tension between a shared objective and unequal access, records the adaptation that preserves the goal, and explains a rejection.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_lower_the_goal","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability","evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_differentiation_is_volume","pattern":"differentiation_as_amount","description":"Equating adapting for variability with giving advanced students more work and struggling students less of the same work","trigger":"teacher's adaptation only changes quantity, leaving the cognitive demand and access identical","feedback":"Adapt the path to the objective, not the page count. More or fewer items rarely changes who can reach the goal.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_lower_the_goal","pattern":"accommodation_confused_with_lowered_expectation","description":"Treating an accommodation as a reason to lower the learning goal rather than to change the route to the same goal","trigger":"teacher swaps the grade-level objective for an easier one when providing support","feedback":"An accommodation changes access, not the target. Keep the objective; change the scaffold, format, or time.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_label_drives_plan","pattern":"deficit_label_as_plan","description":"Adapting based on a category label such as ELL or IEP rather than the specific barrier the student faces in this task","trigger":"teacher applies a generic accommodation tied to a label without naming the task-specific barrier","feedback":"Name the barrier this task creates for this student, then adapt to that barrier. Labels are not lesson plans.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_5_adapting_plans_for_learner_variability_ai_rewrite_preserves_goal","pattern":"ai_simplification_assumed_faithful","description":"Assuming an AI text simplification preserved the cognitive demand when it actually stripped out the complexity the objective asked students to analyze.","trigger":"teacher assigns an AI-simplified source without comparing its demand to the original","feedback":"Compare the adapted task's demand to the original before trusting it: easier reading is not the same thinking. If the objective asks students to analyze competing causes and the simplification removed all but one cause, the goal was quietly lowered in disguise.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"c58082c0186222823b34da86260c211c774d411f972ec165b6243450ee68f3d0","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","me